          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definitions"} Definitions
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#AlgebraicDefinition"} Algebraic definition
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_classical_mechanics"} In classical mechanics
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_geometric_quantization"} In geometric quantization
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_hilbertspace_quantum_mechanics"} In Hilbert-space quantum mechanics
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_aqft"} In AQFT
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_fqft"} In FQFT
              %li
                %a{:href => "#pure_and_mixed_states"} Pure and mixed states
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em state
            of a system in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is a description of everything that is true (or known, or relevant, depending on the circumstances) about the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
          %p
            In the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bayesian+interpretation+of+physics"} Bayesian interpretation of physics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"} pure state
            gives maximal information about the system (which amounts to complete information in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            but not generally in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+state"} mixed state
            is more general. A mixed state can be decomposed into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+distribution"} probability distribution
            on the space of pure states, although this decomposition is unique only for classical systems. In a frequentist interpretation of probability, a mixed state can describe only a statistical ensemble of systems; the real world is in one (generally unknown) pure state (possibly with additional hidden variables in the quantum case, depending on the interpretation of quantum physics).
          %p
            States in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
            describe the state of the system at any given time and are subject to
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                time evolution
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/time+evolution"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
            a single state describes the entire history of the system (with time evolution applying to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            instead).
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+physics"} statistical physics
            %span.newWikiWord
              microstate
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/microstate"} ?
            is a complete description of all of the particles in a system, while a
            %span.newWikiWord
              macrostate
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/macrostate"} ?
            considers only the macroscopic properties known to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            The precise mathematical notion of
            %em state
            depends on what mathematical formalization of mechanics is used.
          %h3#AlgebraicDefinition Algebraic definition
          %p
            Quite generally, both in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            as well as in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+values"} expectation values
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            in an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+observables"} algebra of observables
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+probability+theory"} quantum probability theory
            (which subsumes ordinary
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probability theory
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+in+AQFT+and+operator+algebra"} state in AQFT and operator algebra
          %p for details.
          %h3#in_classical_mechanics In classical mechanics
          %p
            In classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+mechanics"} Lagrangian mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"} point
            in the
            %span.newWikiWord
              state space
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/state+space"} ?
            of the system, giving all of the (generalised)
            %span.newWikiWord
              positions
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/generalized+position"} ?
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/velocity"} velocities
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"} Hamiltonian mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of the system, giving the positions and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momenta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+distribution"} probability distribution
            on the space of pure states.
          %p
            More generally, a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+state"} classical state
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho\colon A \to \mathbb{R}
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            underlying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanical+system"} classical mechanical system
            which satisfies
            %em positivity
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em normalization
          %h3#in_geometric_quantization In geometric quantization
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+states+%28in+geometric+quantization%29"} space of states (in geometric quantization)
          %h3#in_hilbertspace_quantum_mechanics In Hilbert-space quantum mechanics
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"} pure state
            is a ray in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            but
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo ∖
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (H \setminus \{0\})/\mathbb{C}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(H)/\mathrm{U}(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrix"} density matrix
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
          %h3#in_aqft In AQFT
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            is given by a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            is usually defined as a linear function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho\colon A \to \mathbb{C}
            which satisfies
            %em positivity
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %em normalization
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states+in+AQFT+and+operator+algebra"} states in AQFT and operator algebra
          %p
            Arguably, the correct notion of state to use is that of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-state"} quasi-state
            %em is
            a state (at least if the Hilbert space is not of very low dimension, by
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr+topos"} Bohr topos
            for a discussion of this point.
          %h3#in_fqft In FQFT
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            formulation of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                    %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Bord_n^S \to \mathcal{C}
                  \\,.
          %p
            In this formulation the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-morphism"} (n-1)-morphism
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            assigned to an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{n-1}
            is the
            %em space of states
            over that manifold. A state is accordingly a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} generalized element
            of this object.
          %h2#pure_and_mixed_states Pure and mixed states
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+physics"} statistical physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"} pure state
            is a state of maximal information, while a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+state"} mixed state
            is a state with less than maximal information. In the classical case, we may say that a pure state is a state of
            %em complete
            information, but this does not work in the quantum case; from the perspective of the information-theoretic or Bayesian interpretation of quantum physics, this inability to have complete information, even when having maximal information, is the key feature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            that distinguishes it from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+state"} pure state
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p Here are some toy examples of spaces of states.
          %p
            For an impossible system, the space of states is
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
          %p
            For a classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bit"} bit
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+segment"} line segment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi t
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq t \leq 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is the probability that the system is in the first state, with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 - t
            the probability that it is in the second; the two pure states correspond to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = 0
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = 1
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,1)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,0)
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+space"} Lebesgue space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi l
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l^1(2)
          %p
            For a quantum bit, a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qubit"} qubit
            = succeed "-by-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            complex matrix
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi a
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                            %mo −
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                            %mi c
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi b
                            %mo +
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                            %mi c
                          %mtd
                            %mi d
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \begin{pmatrix} a &amp; b - \mathrm{i} c \\ b + \mathrm{i} c &amp; d \end{pmatrix}
          %p
            with unit trace (so
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 1 - a
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi b
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^2 + b^2 + c^2 \leq a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            is misleading, so we use
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi w
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi a
                  %mo −
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi a
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w \coloneqq a - d = 2 a - 1
            instead, along with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ≔
                  %mn 2
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \coloneqq 2 b
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ≔
                  %mn 2
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \coloneqq 2 c
            to keep the same normalization. Then the density matrix may be written
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                            %mo +
                            %mi w
                          %mtd
                            %mi u
                            %mo −
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                            %mi v
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi u
                            %mo +
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                            %mi v
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                            %mo −
                            %mi w
                    %mo )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} I
                  %mo +
                  %mi u
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi v
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %mi w
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac 1 2 \begin{pmatrix} 1 + w &amp; u - \mathrm{i} v \\ u + \mathrm{i} v &amp; 1 - w \end{pmatrix} = \frac 1 2 (\mathrm{I} + u \sigma_x + v \sigma_y + w \sigma_z) ,
          %p
            where the
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+matrices"} Pauli matrices
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi u
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi v
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi w
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ≤
                  %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^2 + v^2 + w^2 \leq 1 .
          %p
            The pure states are those satisfying
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi u
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi v
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi w
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^2 + v^2 + w^2 = 1
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            %span.newWikiWord
              Bloch sphere
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Bloch+sphere"} ?
            (so the full space of all states is the Bloch ball).
